Let $f_\tau:(\mathbb{C}^n,\mathbb{R}^n)\to(\mathbb{C},\mathbb{R})$, $\tau\in(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)$, be a one-parameter family of real analytic functions (or polynomials) realizing a Morse birth surgery, namely:

(i) for every $\tau\in(-\varepsilon,0)$, $f_\tau$ has, in a fixed neighborhood $U\subset\mathbb{C}^n$ of the origin, exactly two non-real critical points which are complex conjugate to each other;

(ii) as $\tau\to 0^-$ these two critical points collide at a single real degenerate critical point of type $A_2$;

(iii) for every $\tau\in(0,\varepsilon)$, $f_\tau$ has in $U$ exactly two real Morse critical points whose Morse indices are two consecutive integers $k$ and $k+1$.

For $\tau<0$, the two complex-conjugate vanishing cycles in the Milnor fiber of $f_\tau$ at the conjugate critical points carry a well-defined intersection number whose sign determines the parity of the larger Morse index $k+1$ of the newborn real critical points.

Problem. Find a topological invariant of the function $f_{-\varepsilon}$ (e.g.\ a characteristic constructed from its real or complex topology, vanishing cycles, monodromy, or discriminant data) which determines the integer Morse indices $k$ and $k+1$ of the two real critical points born at $\tau=0^+$, and not merely the parity of the larger one.

